// What a monotonic reading looks like to somebody inspecting a program, and why it has no reader's form.
//
// `Instant` implements `Debug` and deliberately does not implement `Display`. The absence is the design, not an
// omission waiting to be filled in. `Display` is the text a value has for a reader, and a reading of the monotonic
// clock has none: it is a count from an origin the system chose at boot, it means nothing in another process, and
// it names no moment anybody can look up. A reader handed "812349871234" learns nothing true. What is meaningful is
// the difference between two readings, and that difference is a `Duration`, which renders itself.
//
// So the only rendering here is the diagnostic one. It shows the tick count, because that is the only thing the
// value holds, and it wraps it in the type's name and the unit so that nobody reads it as a moment: `Instant(...)`
// is visibly a reading of something rather than a time, and `nanoseconds:` says what the number counts. Debug
// output is diagnostic and promises no round trip, which is exactly the right promise for a number whose meaning
// ends with the process that produced it.

import Core::{ Result, Unit };
import Text::{ Debug, FormatError, FormatSpec, RejectStyle, TextWriter, WriteAscii, WriteBytes };

extend Instant : Debug {
    /// Writes the reading as `Instant(nanoseconds: N)`.
    ///
    /// The tick count is shown as it is held, in the unit it is held in, named and wrapped so it cannot be mistaken
    /// for a moment on any calendar. There is no `Display` counterpart: a monotonic reading has no text for a
    /// reader, only a difference from another reading, and that difference is a `Duration`.
    ///
    /// # Failures
    /// Reports whatever the writer reported, and `UnsupportedRequest` for any style or precision — a reading has
    /// one form, and neither a style nor a fraction has anything to select here.
    ///
    /// @param writer the destination
    /// @param spec what the placeholder asked for
    /// @returns success when the whole reading was accepted
    /// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/time/instant/write-debug
    pub func WriteDebug(self: &Instant, writer: &var TextWriter, spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
        RejectStyle(spec)?;
        if spec.HasPrecision() { return Result::Error<Unit, FormatError>(FormatError::UnsupportedRequest); }
        WriteBytes(writer, "Instant(nanoseconds: ")?;
        WriteUnsigned(writer, self.ticks)?;
        return WriteAscii(writer, c8')');
    }
}
